WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.940
Path traversal prevention should be tested at the level of the design.

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:12.821
Unit tests can cover path construction, identifier validation, base-
directory containment, missing files, invalid names, and storage adapter
behavior.

3
00:00:12.821 --> 00:00:22.236
Integration tests can exercise upload and download endpoints, object-level
authorization, tenant boundaries, generated reports, and background file
workflows.

4
00:00:22.236 --> 00:00:30.716
Negative tests should confirm that unsafe path-shaped inputs are rejected
or treated as ordinary data without demonstrating offensive techniques.

5
00:00:30.716 --> 00:00:34.656
Code review should follow file-related data from entry to operation.

6
00:00:34.656 --> 00:00:40.666
Reviewers should ask where the filename, ID, archive entry, storage key,
or temporary location came from.

7
00:00:40.666 --> 00:00:55.823
They should check whether the application controls the base directory,
whether containment is enforced, whether authorization happens before
access, whether framework helpers are used correctly, and whether errors
avoid leaking sensitive paths or file contents.

8
00:00:55.823 --> 00:00:59.229
Review should include jobs and scripts, not only web routes.

9
00:00:59.229 --> 00:01:03.903
Logging and monitoring should support investigation without creating new
leaks.

10
00:01:03.903 --> 00:01:11.849
Logs may record safe event names, file IDs, storage areas, tenant context,
authorization outcomes, error categories, and correlation IDs.

11
00:01:11.849 --> 00:01:19.061
They should avoid secrets, tokens, credentials, private file contents,
full sensitive paths, and unnecessary customer data.

12
00:01:19.061 --> 00:01:28.476
Monitoring can watch for unusual file access errors, repeated denied
downloads, unexpected archive failures, or access attempts outside normal
workflow patterns.

13
00:01:28.476 --> 00:01:31.548
If files are exposed, remediation should be concrete.

14
00:01:31.548 --> 00:01:36.222
Identify which files, users, tenants, logs, and storage locations were
affected.

15
00:01:36.222 --> 00:01:38.826
Revoke or rotate exposed secrets when needed.

16
00:01:38.826 --> 00:01:42.298
Disable unsafe routes or workflows while fixes are prepared.

17
00:01:42.298 --> 00:01:49.310
Redesign storage references, add containment checks, strengthen
authorization, clean up logs, and add regression tests.

18
00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:55.720
Prevention must be maintained as file workflows, frameworks, storage
services, and deployment layouts evolve.
